Part 2: The Input-Process-Output (IPO) Model
Think about the fundamental flow of your program.
1. The Core Goal: Every program has three main phases: taking input, processing
data, and presenting output. In one sentence, what is the ultimate goal your
program will accomplish for the user? Be specific and use CS1 concepts.
This program will help the user track the list of shows or movies they meant to watch and
can also include ratings of the movies/shows.
2. Input: What specific information will the user need to provide to your program?
(e.g., a city name, a numerical budget, a list of hobbies).
They will need to provide the movie's name, its rating, and whether or not they have
watched it yet. Also optionally if they would like to include their name to get a more personal
experience
3. Processing: At a high level, how will the computer manipulate or evaluate that
data? (e.g., calculate the average, filter out options that are too expensive,
compare the input against a database).
It takes in text and then makes sure the text isn't repeated as well as taking in a number 1-5
in a drop down box.
4. Output: What will the user get back? (e.g., a recommendation, a calculated
safety factor, a formatted schedule).
The program will return a formatted schedule in a way of all the movies and shows they
have watched and will be able to add and remove as they wish
