Part 7: LLM Conversation
If you used an LLM in any part of this work, copy and paste every part of that
conversation below. Otherwise, you may be given a zero for the assignment. Obviously,
these words will not all be yours, and this is the exception to the requirement at the top
of this assignment that all responses be in your own words. If you did not use an LLM to
assist you in this assignment, simply say so below.
I want to build a simple web tool that helps movie watchers track the movies they want to watch
and movies they have watched, what are important things to include in the web tool.
Here are the most important things to include in a movie tracking tool.
Core features
1. Adding movies. A short form with a title, a genre, and a star rating. Fewer fields make
the tool faster to use.
2. A watchlist view. One page that shows every saved movie in a table with its title, genre,
rating, and watched status. If the list is empty, show a friendly message such as “Nothing
here yet. Add a movie!”
3. Watched vs. not watched. Let users mark a movie as watched once they’ve seen it,
and make it easy to tell which movies are still waiting. This is the main purpose of the
tool.
4. Ratings. A simple 1 to 5 star scale so users remember how they felt about a movie.
Showing the rating as stars makes the list easy to scan.
5. Removing movies. Users need to delete a movie they added by mistake or no longer
want.
Reliability and ease of use
6. Input checks. Reject blank titles, block duplicates (ignoring capitalization), and set a
maximum list size.
7. Clear feedback. After every action, show a short message like “Added Alien!” or “Please
enter a title,” so users never wonder whether something worked.
8. Easy navigation. A home page with buttons for adding a movie, viewing the list, and
seeing stats, plus a way back from every page. Greeting the user by name adds a personal
touch.
9. A reset option. A way to clear the list and start over, with a confirmation message.
Insight features
10. Simple statistics.
● The average rating across all movies
● The user’s top-rated movie
● How many movies they have watched out of the total
● How many movies are in each genre, so users can see their taste
Optional extras
● Filter the list by genre or watched status.
● Suggest the highest-rated unwatched movie as the next pick.
● Record the date a movie was watched, or add personal notes.
The first five items make up the core tool. The rest make it more dependable and more fun to
use.
what are some prior apps that do similar things
Searched the web
Letterboxd is a film diary and social network. You can see your friends’ watchlists, diaries,
likes, and reviews, and it’s best for film culture rather than as a full TV tracker. It’s the closest
match to your idea, since users log films, rate them, and keep lists. fossbytes
moviebase
Trakt is the tracker for serious users. It’s recommended for detailed movie and episode history,
progress tracking, calendars, notifications, and imports. You can add titles to a watchlist, mark
them as watched, or add them to a collection, which is the same watched/unwatched idea as
your site. documentarytube
fossbytes
JustWatch focuses on streaming. You add titles to one synced watchlist, mark them as seen,
and check which streaming service carries them. Its strength is answering “where can I stream
this?” techdogs
Others worth mentioning
● Moviebase is an Android tracker for movies and TV, with Trakt sync, free statistics, and
episode notifications. moviebase
●
● TV Time is a social tracker, with episode comments, badges, and gamification. moviebase
●
● IMDb has its own watchlist feature, and alternatives lists often name it as the top option.
Alternativeto
what is a complicated feature you mentioned that might not work in an introductory cs class
in terms of the actual idea not the example sites
Looking only at the features in your movie tracker idea, and not the example apps, the most
complicated one is recording the date a movie was watched.
Why it’s the hardest
● Dates need a tool outside the basics of CS1, such as Python’s datetime module, or
you’d have to parse and validate text like “03/14/2026” yourself (checking the month
is 1 to 12, the day fits the month, leap years, and so on).
● If the program fills in “today” automatically, unit testing gets awkward, because the
answer changes every day.
● Sorting or comparing dates adds more steps, and using an outside module would
trigger the requirement to document advanced techniques.
Next most complicated: “suggest what to watch next”
This one is doable but trickier than the core features. You have to filter to unwatched
movies, find the highest rating among them, and decide what happens in a tie or when
every movie has been watched. It uses only loops and conditionals, so it fits CS1, but it
needs careful edge-case handling and good tests.
Features that fit CS1 comfortably
● Adding, removing, and rating movies
● Marking movies as watched
● Input checks, such as blank titles, duplicates, and a size limit
● Status messages
● Statistics such as the average rating, the top-rated movie, and genre counts
● A reset button
● Filtering by genre or watched status, which needs a state field to remember the filter,
plus a loop and an if
● Personal notes, which are just another text field
If a question asks which feature you left out and why, you can say you skipped dates
because they need tools or validation that go beyond the course material, and kept the site
focused on features built with lists, loops, and conditionals.
